        <body>
            <div xml:id="d209">

                <pb facs="0443" n="415" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v23p2/pg415"/>

                <head>209. Memorandum to the President’s Special Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Cutler)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d209-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1957-03-29"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29,
                        1957</hi>.</date></dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>New Equipment for U.S. Forces in Korea</item>
                </list>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In response to your letter of March 16, 1957<anchor n="2" xml:id="d209-f2"
                        /> we attach a list of proposed new equipment for U.S. Forces in
                        Korea.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Department of Defense and the Joint Chiefs of Staff believe that, in
                        consonance with U.S. policy to provide U.S. Forces with a selective and
                        flexible [<hi rendition="#italic">less than 1 line of source text not
                            declassified</hi>] capability, all items on the attached list should now
                        be approved for the modernization of U.S. Forces in Korea. [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">2½ lines of source text not
                        declassified</hi>]</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The Department of State believes that under a liberal interpretation of
                        Article 13(d) of the Armistice Agreement we may replace obsolete and
                        worn-out equipment with new items “as close in effectiveness and type as
                        consistent with modern standards of production”. [<hi rendition="#italic">2½
                            lines of source text not declassified</hi>] Accordingly, the Department
                        has been willing to approve all items on the attached list except two: the
                        280 mm “Atomic Cannon” and the 762 mm “Honest John” atomic rocket.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Robert R.
                                Bowie</hi></signed><anchor n="3" xml:id="d209-f3"
                        /><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Assistant Secretary of
                            State</hi><lb/><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                                >Mansfield D. Sprague</hi></signed><lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Assistant Secretary of Defense
                            (ISA)</hi><lb/><signed><hi rendition="#bold">F.W.
                        Farrell</hi></signed><lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Lt. General,
                        USA<lb/>Special Assistant to the JCS for
                            NSC Affairs</hi></closer>



                <xi:include href="d209-a1.xml" xpointer="d209-a1"/>
            </div>
